  "question": {
    "text": "If the value of the integral $\\int_{-1}^1 \\frac{\\cos \\alpha x}{1+3^x} d x$ is $\\frac{2}{\\pi}$. Then, a value of $\\alpha$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "hUG1D",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "uduc8",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{6}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ZLSdF",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{4}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "xq3WG",
      "text": "$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $I=\\int_{-1}^{+1} \\frac{\\cos \\alpha x}{1+3^x} d x$ ...(I)\n$I=\\int_{-1}^{+1} \\frac{\\cos \\alpha x}{1+3^{-x}} d x$\n$\\left(u \\operatorname{sing} \\int_a^b f(x) d x=\\int_a^b f(a+b-x) d x\\right)$ ...(II)\nAdd (1) and (II)\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& 2 I=\\int_{-1}^{+1} \\cos (\\alpha x) d x=2 \\int_0^1 \\cos (\\alpha x) d x \\\\\n& I=\\frac{\\sin \\alpha}{\\alpha}=\\frac{2}{\\pi}(\\text { given }) \\\\\n& \\therefore \\alpha=\\frac{\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}$",
    "image": null
  },
